    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under COSO Principle 15 (External Communication), an organization communicates with external parties regarding matters affecting the functioning of internal control. SOC 1 Type 2 reports provide assurance over a service provider's controls during a specified period — but when the report identifies an exception, management cannot simply accept the report and take no action. The SOC report is not a seal of approval when exceptions exist; rather, it is information that management must evaluate. Greenway has two responsibilities under COSO Principle 15: (1) obtain sufficient evidence that the exception has been remediated — this requires more than the provider's verbal assertion; a bridge letter covering the period from the SOC report date to the current date, an updated SOC report, or management's direct testing of the provider's controls are appropriate; and (2) evaluate the severity of the deficiency on Greenway's own financial reporting. A segregation-of-duties failure in billing adjustments and write-offs directly affects the completeness and accuracy of accounts receivable, a significant balance in Greenway's financial statements. Until verified remediation is confirmed, Greenway should implement compensating controls — such as reviewing billing adjustment reports, reconciling write-offs to supporting documentation, and performing trend analysis on receivables balances — to mitigate the risk that errors or irregularities go undetected. Business interpretation: reliance on a service organization's SOC report does not relieve user entity management of its responsibility to evaluate identified exceptions and protect the integrity of its own financial reporting. Exam trap: candidates sometimes treat a SOC report as a binary pass/fail instrument; in practice, the report is an input to management's assessment of control risk, and management retains the obligation to respond to exceptions.",
