    "ExplanationWrongA": "Accepting the provider's statement at face value violates COSO Principle 15's requirement that external communication be evaluated for sufficiency. A SOC 1 Type 2 report with an identified exception is not a clean opinion — it is a qualified or adverse conclusion on the control for the affected period. The provider's unverified assertion, without evidence, does not satisfy management's responsibility to obtain sufficient information about whether the deficiency was actually corrected. Furthermore, COSO Principle 10 requires control activities that mitigate identified risks; accepting an unverified claim of remediation leaves Greenway exposed to the same control weakness for any billing adjustments and write-offs processed since the exception period. The independence of the service auditor does not excuse management from its own obligation to evaluate the impact on Greenway's financial reporting and to ensure that risks to the integrity of accounts receivable are addressed through evidence-based follow-up rather than reliance on an unsupported statement from the service provider.",
