    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under COSO Principle 16 (Ongoing and Separate Evaluations), the organization selects, develops, and performs ongoing and/or separate evaluations to ascertain whether the components of internal control are present and functioning. COSO Principle 16 does not prescribe a single monitoring method; it explicitly contemplates a mix tailored to circumstances. The key factors in determining the appropriate monitoring approach are: (1) Risk level — high-risk areas such as Dalton's cash reconciliation and wire transfers warrant more frequent, near-real-time monitoring because the consequences of failure are severe and immediate. (2) Nature of the control — automated controls embedded in systems are naturally suited to continuous monitoring, while manual controls requiring human judgment (e.g., physical inventory cycle counts where an observer must identify damaged or obsolete stock) are better suited to separate evaluations at defined intervals. (3) Rate of change — areas experiencing rapid change (new product lines, system implementations, regulatory shifts) require more frequent monitoring than stable, mature processes. (4) Cost-benefit trade-off — the cost of implementing continuous automated monitoring (software, integration, maintenance) must be weighed against the benefit of earlier detection; for Dalton's semi-annual access reviews in a stable organizational structure, the incremental benefit of continuous monitoring may not justify its cost. Business interpretation: monitoring is not one-size-fits-all; effective internal control allocates monitoring resources to areas where the risk-adjusted benefit of timely detection is highest.",
