    "ExplanationWrongA": "Attributing the failure to COSO Principle 14 (internal communication) misidentifies the root cause. While communication is a component of effective internal control — management should communicate remediation status to relevant parties — the deficiency at Ridgeway is not primarily a communication failure. The core issues are: (1) management self-certified remediation without independent verification (5 of 11 remediations were ineffective), (2) no tracking mechanism existed for in-progress items (3 items with no updates in 8 months), and (3) the CEO believes self-assessment is sufficient. This is a deficiency assessment, tracking, and verification failure under COSO Principle 17, not a communication failure under Principle 14. Issuing a company-wide memo on control compliance — the proposed fix — would not establish independent verification of remediation effectiveness, create a formal tracking system with assigned owners and target dates, or provide the audit committee with reliable information about whether control weaknesses have been resolved. A memo communicates; it does not verify, track, or remediate. Principle 17 specifically requires that the organization track whether deficiencies are remediated on a timely basis — a tracking system, not a memo.",
